  "context_policy": {
    "include_when": "Include information directly when it materially changes interpretation, execution, verification, authority selection, scope, or a near-term decision and is sufficiently stable/compact to remain useful in context. Include information when it materially changes interpretation, authority selection, scope, state, transition, execution, verification, or return semantics.",
    "exclude_when": "Exclude information that is merely related, duplicative, obsolete, decorative, or incapable of changing any permitted decision, except where retained as labeled historical evidence. Exclude material that is redundant, purely decorative, obsolete, or unrelated to any consequential decision, unless retained as clearly separated reference material for a stated reason.",
    "retrieve_on_demand_when": "Retrieve information when it is large, mutable, authoritative, or only conditionally relevant and an available interface can fetch the needed portion at the decision point. Retrieve large, volatile, authoritative, or rarely needed context when a specific decision depends on it and the source is available at execution time.",
    "locality_rule": "At each consequential decision, the executor must have a direct local representation or unambiguous retrievable reference to the rules, state, authority, and constraints governing that decision. Do not rely on distant context whose applicability must be reconstructed. Place or link governing information close enough to the decision it controls that the executor need not infer which distant rule applies; use identifiers/cross-references rather than duplicating divergent prose.",
    "signal_density_rule": "Preserve all information that can materially change correctness; remove or defer information that cannot. Repetition is justified only when needed to preserve a critical rule across context boundaries or decision locality. Prefer the smallest context set that preserves all materially consequential semantics; never delete information solely to shorten the instruction when deletion increases execution ambiguity or error risk."
  },
